ve in sub-Saharan Africa, with a small remnant in Iran.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7959-8310-845e4322d5dc","description":"Africa","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-73d1-8436-a66c67ac6b4e","description":"Australia","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7e1a-b6b4-7b8d9135ae10","description":"Europe","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-797d-a585-f28e4e5d642b","description":"South America","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7076-b411-c8711726bb4d","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What are the first digits of pi?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"3.14 is a common approximation of pi, whose decimal digits continue forever without repeating.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-77f5-9565-b8b541dfb6e6","description":"3.14","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7ad5-b72f-edb980a6e91a","description":"3.41","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7fd6-b39f-416b67dd97e2","description":"4.31","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-754e-8f52-569e3280df38","description":"1.34","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-707f-b3da-39ae6384694f","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What is used as a disinfectant in swimming pools?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"Chlorine helps kill harmful microbes in pool water. The familiar strong pool odor often comes from chloramines formed when chlorine reacts with contaminants.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7e0d-b124-80261780b37f","description":"Chlorine","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7c58-938c-f83709803c8e","description":"Sulfur","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7cb1-b2dc-e0199b944b2b","description":"Carbon","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-73a7-a33d-c75ec0a5072c","description":"Cobalt","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7a09-adc0-6f6939c1e208","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What is used by submarines to observe the surface from a concealed position?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"A periscope redirects light, letting someone see over an obstruction while staying concealed.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7b33-8d17-d87be7079845","description":"Periscope","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-76c0-a18c-72db74fbf0ad","description":"Episcope","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7908-8b25-14df7f636903","description":"Stroboscope","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7a69-91d3-7ddc6e2cfbff","description":"Stethoscope","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7944-a27b-e303ff0755a5","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What do herbivores eat?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"Herbivores eat plants, and many have teeth or digestive systems suited to grazing or browsing.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-716c-a1da-9f374185f6b7","description":"Plants","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7ee7-b3d1-093e21e65278","description":"Beetles","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7972-97be-2930209586ee","description":"Snails","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7e0d-95c0-1de4f99b9ec3","description":"Fish","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-73ac-a53e-16f94e851258","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"The cranium, which encloses the brain, is part of which larger bony structure?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"The skull includes both the cranium, which protects the brain, and the facial bones.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7b7d-8af9-72d34ed9ebb3","description":"Skull","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7a3f-ab63-7d809fecc471","description":"Pelvis","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-74e2-b7d6-2c437894c22d","description":"Spine","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7ab8-8f75-d5d7b11813cc","description":"Ribcage","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7144-871e-1aadc020c809","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"Who developed the theory of evolution by natural selection?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"Charles Darwin set out natural selection in On the Origin of Species (1859), developed after his voyage on HMS Beagle.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-761a-9e92-21993f5af9ed","description":"Charles Darwin","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7508-8456-8fe5d4c4113f","description":"Albert Einstein","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7b25-accb-9ca56acbd9a9","description":"Isaac Newton","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7468-ad90-96b68aeee71a","description":"Galileo Galilei","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7f31-82db-a1e836b3cc45","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"Which term refers to objects or lines that are side by side and will never intersect?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"Parallel lines stay the same distance apart; perpendicular lines meet at a right angle, while convergent lines move toward one another.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7369-8626-c4cac0b353e3","description":"Parallel","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7ae6-9d96-18a5336c11c7","description":"Perpendicular","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-717d-a60e-e5ba7a35e6f4","description":"Diagonal","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-75c2-a715-bae8fa50968d","description":"Convergent","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7ac7-92ec-4b78ae0da948","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"Marine iguanas, saltwater crocodiles, sea snakes and sea turtles all belong to which class of animals?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"Reptiles are air-breathing vertebrates with scales. Sea turtles must surface for air, while marine iguanas can hold their breath underwater.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-740d-8ba6-11d4c5a1a8ad","description":"Reptiles","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-77e7-91af-b0a22c81cecb","description":"Mammals","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7766-9d1f-cf11522c6a4d","description":"Amphibians","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7251-bfd4-4b9955e5fde6","description":"Fish","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-75a9-8884-dc48028b0a51","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What would you call a baby owl?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"An owlet is a young owl; the ending -let often marks something small or young, as in piglet.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-720e-a015-cf16cc9abdfb","description":"An Owlet","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-77db-b108-8c1072d48a61","description":"A Caterpillar","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-71e5-836e-cb6513ad5baf","description":"A Codling","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-73b4-9858-915bf11e76c1","description":"A Colt","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-785a-a68e-dbc6f27158f6","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"A young butterfly is known as what?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"A caterpillar is a butterfly\'s larval stage; after growing, it forms a chrysalis and later emerges as an adult.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-75f6-87a8-bf630f392c99","description":"A Caterpillar","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7e3d-b682-8f584950a23e","description":"A Codling","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7472-ab93-bd904493e66c","description":"A Chick","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-71c4-943c-02a0a5ad90fd","description":"A Spiderling","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-74bc-b3b3-8775d4b0ab6b","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What type of tree do both dates and coconuts come from?","difficulty":1,"explanation":"Date palms and coconut palms are different members of the palm family, which also includes many ornamental and oil-producing plants.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7b25-92ce-212411442861","description":"Palm tree","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a21904-ad4e-7d7d-8f87-a487e10011e5","description":"Maple tree","